# START HERE — pxa @@TAG@@

You have never built this project and don't need to. This package is a finished binary
release: untar it, get a model, run one command.

## Requirements

- **Linux, x86_64.** (No macOS/Windows binaries in this package.)
- **A recent-enough Linux distro: glibc >= @@GLIBC_FLOOR@@.** Concretely: @@DISTRO_HINT@@.
  Check yours with `ldd --version` (first line). This is a real hard floor, not a "should work
  on anything" guess: a binary from this same codebase, built against a newer glibc, was tested
  by hand against an older distro and failed to even start ("version GLIBC_2.38 not found") --
  an older-than-@@GLIBC_FLOOR@@ host will fail the exact same way, and there is no workaround
  short of a distro upgrade or a container with a new-enough userspace. glibc itself is the one
  thing this package cannot safely bundle (same reason it can't bundle the NVIDIA driver: it has
  to match what the kernel/loader on your box already has). libstdc++ does NOT add a second
  constraint here -- this package bundles its own copy in `lib/` (see GLIBCXX_FLOOR in VERSION
  for exactly which one) and the wrapper scripts put it ahead of your system's on the library
  search path, so only the glibc floor above matters.
- **An NVIDIA GPU from the Pascal or Volta generation** — e.g. GTX 10-series / Tesla P100
  (Pascal, "sm_60"/"sm_61") or Tesla V100 (Volta, "sm_70"). This build was compiled for exactly
  those three architectures; it will not run GPU kernels on a Turing/Ampere/Ada/Hopper/Blackwell
  card (it may still run on CPU, slowly — see docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md).
- **An NVIDIA driver new enough for the bundled CUDA @@CUDA_MAJOR_MINOR@@ runtime.** The
  documented floor for CUDA @@CUDA_MAJOR_MINOR@@ is driver **@@DRIVER_FLOOR@@** or newer on
  Linux. Check yours with `nvidia-smi` (top-right corner shows "Driver Version"). This package
  was built and tested on driver @@BUILD_HOST_DRIVER@@.
- Enough free disk + VRAM for whichever model tier you download (see step 2).
- `python3` on your PATH (used only to run the launcher script; nothing to `pip install`).

@@PATCHELF_NOTE@@

## Three steps

### 1. Untar

```bash
tar xzf pxa-@@TAG@@-linux-x86_64-cuda@@CUDA_MAJOR_MINOR@@-*.tar.gz
cd pxa-@@TAG@@
```

### 2. Get a model

Models are not bundled (they're tens of GB). Download one GGUF file from one of these two
Hugging Face repos — both are named, with exact file hashes, in
`bench/fair/weights/MANIFEST.sha256`:

- **`huggingface.co/poisonxa/PXA-Fusion2-35B-GGUF`** — the flagship MoE model this fork is
  tuned for. Start with the smallest tier, `PXA-Fusion2-35B-PXQ2.gguf` (~10 GB), if you have a
  single 16 GB card; step up to `-PXQ3` or `-PXQ6` if you have more VRAM. This is the model
  `docs/COOKBOOK.md`'s recipes are written against.
- **`huggingface.co/poisonxa/Qwable-27B-GGUF`** — a smaller dense 27B model, useful if you want
  something that fits on one card at higher quality per GB (`Qwable-27B-PXQ4core.gguf`).

Verify what you downloaded against `bench/fair/weights/MANIFEST.sha256` before running it:

```bash
cd bench/fair/weights && sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256 --ignore-missing && cd -
```

### 3. Run

```bash
./pxa-launch -m /path/to/the/model/you/downloaded.gguf
```

`pxa-launch` looks at your GPU(s) and the model file and picks a working configuration,
printing the exact command it runs before running it (see `docs/LAUNCHER.md` if present, or
`tools/pxa-launch.py --help` / `--explain`). If you'd rather run `llama-server` directly with
your own flags, use `./run-server.sh` instead — same wrapper, no auto-tuning:

```bash
./run-server.sh -m /path/to/model.gguf -c 4096 -ngl 99 --port 8080
```

A correct startup prints a line starting `PXA level=...` on stderr near the top of the log —
that's this fork's engine identifying itself and its per-device configuration. If you don't see
it, you're not running this fork's binary (check `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` / RPATH — see
"libcuda not found" below).

## Troubleshooting (five most likely failures)

1. **"CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version" / server refuses to start
   on GPU.** Your NVIDIA driver is older than @@DRIVER_FLOOR@@. Run `nvidia-smi` and check the
   driver version; upgrade the driver (not CUDA — this package brings its own CUDA runtime).

2. **Starts, but is very slow or crashes on a kernel.** Your card is not Pascal or Volta (see
   Requirements). This build was not compiled for other architectures. `nvidia-smi
   --query-gpu=name --format=csv` will tell you the card name; check it against
   Pascal/Volta model lists if unsure.

3. **"failed to allocate buffer" / out-of-memory / server dies while loading weights.** The
   model tier doesn't fit your VRAM (+ context + compute buffer headroom, roughly the model
   file size plus 2-3 GB). Pick a smaller quant tier (e.g. `-PXQ2` instead of `-PXQ6`), lower
   `-c` (context size), or use `-ngl` less than 99 / `--n-cpu-moe` to offload part of the model
   to CPU (see `docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md` for which quant tiers have a fast CPU path).

4. **"version GLIBC_x.y not found" (or similar) when even running `./run-server.sh --version`.**
   Your Linux distro is older than glibc @@GLIBC_FLOOR@@ — see the requirement above (`ldd
   --version` to check). This failure mode is real and confirmed by hand while packaging this
   codebase, not a hypothetical: no flag or
   env var fixes it, only a newer distro or a container with a new-enough userspace does.

5. **"error while loading shared libraries: libcuda.so.1: cannot open shared object file"** (or
   `libnvidia-ml.so`, etc.) **— this means the NVIDIA driver itself is not installed/visible**,
   not a problem with this package. This package intentionally does NOT bundle driver
   libraries (`libcuda.so*`, `libnvidia-*`) — those must come from your host's own NVIDIA driver
   install (or be passed through into a container, e.g. `--runtime=nvidia` /
   `--gpus all` with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit). Confirm the driver is visible with
   `nvidia-smi` before reporting anything else as a bug.

## Where things are

```
bin/            llama-server, llama-cli, llama-bench, llama-perplexity, llama-quantize,
                (llama-gguf-split if this build included it), and three self-test binaries.
lib/            this engine's shared libraries + the exact CUDA/OpenMP runtime libs it needs.
                Driver libraries are deliberately NOT here (see troubleshooting #5).
pxa-launch      run this. Auto-picks a config for your hardware+model, prints its reasoning.
run-server.sh   thin wrapper: sets up ./lib, execs bin/llama-server with your own flags.
tools/pxa-launch.py   what pxa-launch actually runs (kept here so --explain/--selftest work).
bench/fair/     the fair-comparison benchmark harness + protocol.md + weights/MANIFEST.sha256.
bench/gate/     the release gate (correctness/determinism checks) — see bench/gate/README.md.
                Run it as: LD_LIBRARY_PATH=./lib MODEL=/path/to/model.gguf BIN=./bin
                bench/gate/run-gate.sh   (bench/gate calls bin/llama-server directly, so it
                needs ./lib on LD_LIBRARY_PATH itself — pxa-launch/run-server.sh do this for
                you, run-gate.sh does not).
docs/           README, this release's notes, COOKBOOK (per-card recipes), KNOWN-ISSUES,
                LAUNCHER (if shipped with this build).
VERSION         exactly what commit/flags/CUDA version/GPU architectures this build is.
LICENSE, LICENSING.md   MIT-family licenses; LICENSING.md explains which files fall under which.
```
